# AgentCake: Caddy's own JSON access log is all `agentcake logs import` needs (SERVER.md, "Server logs"): it holds
# every request header, the time to the microsecond and the Host. AgentCake finds the format by itself, or takes
# `--format caddy-json`. The log stays yours: AgentCake reads it where it is, never copies it, and keeps nothing of a
# line that matches no visit. Like any access log it holds visitors' addresses: keep Caddy's rolling of it.

example.com {
	log {
		output file /var/log/caddy/agentcake.log
		format json
	}
	# ... the rest of your site, as before
}

# Caddy logs the address it was connected from (request.remote_ip) and, with trusted_proxies set, the visitor's
# (request.client_ip), which AgentCake takes first: it has to be the same one your load balancer sends AgentCake as
# its client IP header. Caddy does not log the values of Cookie and Authorization unless log_credentials is on, and
# AgentCake reads neither in any case.
#
# Then, on this machine, every five minutes (cron), to the AgentCake server's public listener:
#   agentcake logs import /var/log/caddy/agentcake.log --since 10m --to http://192.0.2.10:8080/cake
